          <p dir="auto">Hello.<br>
I have been following the latest comments on G21/G31 and MTP. In case it adds another data point to the discussion, we’ve been testing this PR on an Intel Arc Pro B70, both in target-only inference and with speculative decoding.</p>
<p dir="auto">For target-only we ran a BEFORE → AFTER A/B with llama-server, 5 repetitions per configuration, median reported:</p>
<p dir="auto">Intel Arc Pro B70 — target-only decode</p>
<div class="snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="            Q4_0 KV                  Q8_0 KV"><pre class="notranslate"><code class="notranslate">            Q4_0 KV                  Q8_0 KV
</code></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">-c 8K       26.00 → 27.61  +6.19%    26.95 → 28.00  +3.90%<br>
-c 32K      26.11 → 27.87  +6.72%    26.92 → 28.21  +4.78%<br>
-c 64K      26.43 → 27.79  +5.15%    26.87 → 27.91  +3.84%<br>
-c 128K     26.21 → 27.52  +5.01%    26.98 → 28.01  +3.82%<br>
-c 262K     26.43 → 26.96  +1.98%    26.95 → 28.00  +3.89%</p>
<p dir="auto">F16 KV was essentially neutral as a control:</p>
<p dir="auto">F16 KV<br>
64K     29.55 → 29.51   -0.12%<br>
128K    29.58 → 29.51   -0.22%</p>
<p dir="auto">We also temporarily implemented the dispatch. In target-only mode with quantised KV, we observed 6,864/6,864 calls to TILE and 0 to VEC, and FLASH_ATTN_EXT passed 4,001/4,001.</p>
<p dir="auto">A clarification regarding these results: the 8K→262K sweep is context-assigned (-c), using the same short workload, and therefore does not represent an actual KV depth of 262K. Separately, we conducted kernel-level tests by actually varying the KV between 8K and 128K; there, too, we did not observe the TILE advantage diminishing with depth. In Q8_0, for example, TILE outperformed VEC by approximately 21–26% for ne1=1, 46–48% for ne1=2 and ~70% for ne1=3/4.</p>
<p dir="auto">Following the discussion on MTP, we also reviewed our results using speculative decoding. We are not using MTP in these tests, but rather an external DFlash/DFlash2 draft, so I do not consider this to be directly comparable with the MTP results mentioned above. Even so, it may be a useful piece of additional information:</p>
<p dir="auto">B70 — speculative decode, Q8_0 KV, 64K</p>
<div class="snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="               BEFORE    AFTER     delta"><pre class="notranslate"><code class="notranslate">               BEFORE    AFTER     delta
</code></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">DFlash1 n_max=1     32.60     33.72    +3.4%<br>
DFlash1 n_max=2     37.36     36.58    -2.1%  (noise)</p>
<p dir="auto">DFlash2 n_max=1     31.91     33.14    +3.9%<br>
DFlash2 n_max=2     39.65     39.99    +0.8%  (noise)</p>
<p dir="auto">In our case we were able to observe why those results differ. With n_max=1, the dominant verify geometry was Q_ne1=2, where this PR actually changes the dispatch, and we recovered roughly the same +3–4% improvement seen target-only. With n_max=2, the dominant geometry became Q_ne1=3; those calls were already using TILE before applying this PR, so BEFORE and AFTER remained essentially unchanged.</p>
<p dir="auto">DFlash/DFlash2 acceptance rates showed no systematic shift between the two builds.</p>
<p dir="auto">I’m not sure if this is related to the MTP behaviour currently being investigated in G21 — they are different speculative mechanisms — but I wanted to share the full results because, at least in our B70, we have not observed a regression associated with speculative decoding: when the PR actually modifies the path used, we see an improvement, and when that path was already TILE, the result remains neutral.</p>

